 how kingship was perceived for centuries.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-10-24T07:04:19Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#40069994818", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Crimthann Nia Náir was a economist or social scientist of the Classical era, an age of empires, philosophical inquiry, and foundational civilizations, active in Ireland. Known in the fields of ireland, monarchs & rulers, europe.", "newValue": "Crimthann Nia Náir, a legendary High King of Ireland whose reign is traditionally placed between 4 and 9 CE, stands as a pivotal figure in the pre-Christian Gaelic historical cycles. His narrative, deeply embedded in medieval Irish genealogies and chronicles such as the *Lebor Gabála Érenn* and the *Annals of the Four Masters*, embodies the early concept of Irish sovereignty and the mythic connections between Ireland and the wider Celtic world. While historical details remain elusive, his story illustrates the cultural importance of heroic kingship and the acquisition of wealth and prestige through martial prowess and foreign expeditions, reflecting a societal ideal of a powerful, well-traveled ruler.

